no matter how you use Google cloud  platform we have tools to help you get  things done quickly and easily and this  short video will cover the cloud SDK the  g-cloud command line and cloud shell the  cloud console our integrated web-based  Google cloud platform management web UI  is a great place to start using GCP it  offers powerful and centralized site to  manage all of your resources projects  and billing information and you should  check out the video link below for more  details  next we'll talk about the command-line  developer and admin tools let's start  with Cloud SDK cloud SDK is a set of  command line tools including g-cloud GS  util and BQ which you can use to access  compute engine cloud storage bigquery  and other products from the command line  you can run these tools interactively or  in your automated scripts cloud SDK is  supported on Linux Mac OS and Windows  and requires Python to run you can  install via apt-get or yum or by using  an installer and interactive or non  interactive mode once installed the SDK  supports updates and additional features  as we'll see in a minute we'll also talk  about how not to install cloud SDK later  in this video the most used part of  cloud SDK is G cloud and the typical  first command after installation is  g-cloud init to perform setup tasks this  takes care of both authorizing Cloud SDK  tools to use your user account  credentials to access your DCP resources  as well as setting up a configuration  which covers the active account the  current project and optionally the  default compute engine region and zone  you can rerun this command at a later  time to change your settings or to  create a new configuration you can view  the current configuration at any time  using the G cloud config list command  managing and updating Cloud SDK is also  done using G cloud by default the SDK  installs G cloud commands that are  generally available or GA additional  functionality is available as SDK  components named alpha and beta and can  be installed using the G cloud  components installed command updates the  existing components are achieved with  g-cloud components update once installed  and configured you can achieve virtually  anything with GTL that you can do with  cloud console in fact when using cloud  console you're often able to obtain the  equivalent g-cloud command for what you  are doing in the graphical interface  manage VMs and storage buckets setup and  networking and firewalls built and test  locally deploy to production monitor  logs it can all be done with G cloud and  all of this can be automated in scripts  and in first code or CI CD tools for a  richer more interactive experience as  you type Auto completion is available  via an interactive mode check it out  it works with G cloud but also with the  other command line tools in the SDK for  those windows powershell users out there  do not feel left out as we have cloud  tools for powershell this set of command  lets for powershell will let you manage  GCB resources in a way that should feel  familiar let's now switch to cloud shell  wouldn't it be nice if you had an always  available browser-based environment with  g cloud what if it also came with all  your favorite tools such as git bash  built-in package tools docker cube CTL  your language specific tools text  editors etc this is precisely what cloud  shell offers a temporary g1 small  virtual machine running a Debian image  hosted on GCP with all these tools  always up to date it comes with five  gigabytes of persistent disk storage for  your scripts source files dot files and  for your own binaries  there's even a built in web editor with  support for Java Go Python node Ruby PHP  and dotnet core all of this from a  simple web browser best of all it's free  Kyle shell can be used to run g-cloud  commands only without having to install  update or configure it authorization to  GCP projects and resources is also built  in cloud shell can also be used as a  